# Unattended verification — configuration.
#
# Sourced by the engine. Anything already in the environment wins, so a one-off change needs no edit:
#
#   SOAK_MINUTES=5 RUN_MUTATION=false ./scripts/nightly.sh
#
# For settings you want permanently but do not want to commit, put them in
# `scripts/nightly.config.local` (git-ignored) — it is sourced after this file.
#
# ⚠️ This file is `source`d by bash, so it can execute anything. It is a local developer tool, not a
# CI runner accepting untrusted input; keep it to KEY=value and it stays boring.

# ── which stages run ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
RUN_PREFLIGHT=${RUN_PREFLIGHT:-true}   # never turn this off for an unattended run — see docs/nightly/README.md
RUN_GATE=${RUN_GATE:-true}   # format, typecheck, lint, unit tests, build, python
RUN_SOAK=${RUN_SOAK:-true}   # the long stability run
RUN_BENCHMARK=${RUN_BENCHMARK:-true}   # capacity ladder, warm-up, reproducibility → benchmark.md + JSON
RUN_MUTATION=${RUN_MUTATION:-true}   # ⚠️ edits source files and rebuilds images — read the warning below
RUN_DEPLOYMENT=${RUN_DEPLOYMENT:-true}   # every running byte is the committed byte
RUN_RUNTIME=${RUN_RUNTIME:-true}   # real inference end to end, through a camera
RUN_DASHBOARD=${RUN_DASHBOARD:-true}   # the operator page in a real browser
RUN_BOUNDARY=${RUN_BOUNDARY:-true}   # contracts + the perception boundary gate
RUN_INTEGRATION=${RUN_INTEGRATION:-true}   # cross-service integration tests
RUN_REPORT=${RUN_REPORT:-true}   # ⚠️ turning this off means the night produces no summary
RUN_CLEANUP=${RUN_CLEANUP:-true}   # weekly only: prune old runs and reclaim docker build cache

# ── tracking (P-8 Phase 4) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
RUN_TRACKING=${RUN_TRACKING:-true}   # identities through the deployment, against authored ground truth
RUN_TRACKING_DEPLOYMENT=${RUN_TRACKING_DEPLOYMENT:-true}   # tracking is on in the image that is running
RUN_TRACKING_BROWSER=${RUN_TRACKING_BROWSER:-true}   # the three track pages in a real browser
RUN_TRACKING_BENCHMARK=${RUN_TRACKING_BENCHMARK:-true}   # tracks/s, ID stability, lost + recovered, at 1→16 cameras
RUN_TRACKING_MUTATION=${RUN_TRACKING_MUTATION:-true}   # ⚠️ edits source files — see the mutation warning below

# ── the live event bridge (P-8 Phase 5) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
RUN_EVENT_BRIDGE=${RUN_EVENT_BRIDGE:-true}   # a frame becomes an incident candidate, through the whole chain
RUN_EVENT_REPLAY=${RUN_EVENT_REPLAY:-true}   # duplicates, correlation, replay determinism, payload v1/v2/v3
RUN_EVENT_BROWSER=${RUN_EVENT_BROWSER:-true}   # the event bridge page in a real browser
RUN_PUBLISHER_BENCHMARK=${RUN_PUBLISHER_BENCHMARK:-true}   # publish latency, queue, shedding, at 1→16 cameras
RUN_BRIDGE_MUTATION=${RUN_BRIDGE_MUTATION:-true}   # ⚠️ edits source files AND stops the broker — see below
# ⚠️ STOPS NATS on the running deployment. Every service shares that broker, so the whole stack is
# degraded for the outage window on purpose. Off on any machine where that matters more than the answer.
RUN_BROKER_RESILIENCE=${RUN_BROKER_RESILIENCE:-true}

# ── Camera Processing Assignment (P-8 Phase 6) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⚠️ `RUN_ASSIGNMENT` is the one that carries the milestone's central invariant: three cameras
# record, one is assigned, and the other two must analyse NOTHING. Every other stage in this file
# proves the platform CAN analyse a camera; only this one proves it can decline to.
RUN_ASSIGNMENT=${RUN_ASSIGNMENT:-true}                 # selectivity, hot assignment, pause, release, re-enable
RUN_ASSIGNMENT_RUNTIME=${RUN_ASSIGNMENT_RUNTIME:-true} # health, capacity, failover, persistence across restart
RUN_ASSIGNMENT_BROWSER=${RUN_ASSIGNMENT_BROWSER:-true} # the six operator pages, traced to their payloads
RUN_ASSIGNMENT_BENCHMARK=${RUN_ASSIGNMENT_BENCHMARK:-true}  # assignment + runtime latency at 1→16 cameras
RUN_ASSIGNMENT_MUTATION=${RUN_ASSIGNMENT_MUTATION:-true}    # eight breaks, each red at the check naming it

# ⚠️ **EXECUTION POLICY, set 2026-08-06: long verification belongs HERE, not in the working day.**
#
# Any verification expected to exceed ~10–15 minutes is registered as a nightly stage unless it is
# strictly required to validate the implementation in front of you. Capacity ladders, soak tests,
# extended benchmarks, long regression suites and multi-camera endurance runs all qualify.
#
# ⚠️ The obligation is on the milestone that INTRODUCES the run, not on whoever notices later: a long
# benchmark ships with its stage row in the same commit, exactly as an ADR ships with its index row.
# Daytime work runs enough of it to prove correctness — one or two rungs — and the full ladder runs
# here. A milestone may sit at **Pending Freeze** until the nightly result lands; freeze depends on
# those numbers, implementation does not.
#
# See DEFINITION_OF_DONE.md and the verification-economy policy of 2026-08-05, which this extends.

# ── Retail Loitering (P-8 Phase 7) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⚠️ `RUN_LOITERING` is the first stage that proves a COMPLETE CUSTOMER FEATURE rather than a layer.
# Every other stage in this file proves a hop works; this one proves a person standing in a checkout
# queue becomes an incident an operator can act on — and, on the same deployment at the same moment,
# that a camera with no zone never stamps one and a dry-run rule raises nothing while its clock runs.
RUN_LOITERING=${RUN_LOITERING:-true}                   # camera → assignment → identity → zone → candidate
RUN_LOITERING_ZONES=${RUN_LOITERING_ZONES:-true}       # zones travelled on the plan and were evaluated
RUN_RULE_BROWSER=${RUN_RULE_BROWSER:-true}             # zone editor, live rule status, incident detail
RUN_RULE_BENCHMARK=${RUN_RULE_BENCHMARK:-true}         # event→rule, rule→candidate, end-to-end, separated
RUN_RULE_MUTATION=${RUN_RULE_MUTATION:-true}           # eight breaks, each red at the check naming it

# ⚠️ OFF by default, and it should stay off. A machine that commits while you sleep is a machine that
# can push a broken tree into the morning with nobody having read it. The reports are written either
# way; committing them is a decision a person makes.
RUN_COMMIT=${RUN_COMMIT:-false}

# ── how long ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SOAK_MINUTES=${SOAK_MINUTES:-30}   # 15 for a quick check · 30 nightly · 120+ only with a reason (see below)
# ⚠️ TWO, not four, and this is an operating-point choice rather than a weakened check.
# The measured sustainable capacity is 4 cameras on a QUIET host — and it sits right on the 2 %
# frame-loss boundary: 0.4 % dropped one morning, 4.4 % the same evening on the same commit, purely
# from host thermal state. A stability run held at the capacity edge reports the edge, not stability.
# Two cameras dropped 0.0 % under both conditions, so a drop here means something actually changed.
# The weekly profile raises it — measuring the edge is the benchmark's job, not the soak's.
SOAK_CAMERAS=${SOAK_CAMERAS:-2}

# ⚠️ A long soak needs a justification, per the execution policy of 2026-08-05: a suspected memory or
# resource leak, a suspected concurrency or scheduling issue, a release candidate, hardware
# validation (P-9), pilot readiness (P-10), or GA. Otherwise 15–30 minutes is the standard and the
# hours are better spent on features.
SOAK_REASON=${SOAK_REASON:-""}   # required when SOAK_MINUTES > 60; pre-flight refuses without it

# ── per-stage timeouts, in seconds. 0 = no limit ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⚠️ Every long stage has one. Without them a single hang costs the entire night and produces
# nothing, which is the one outcome this framework exists to prevent.
TIMEOUT_PREFLIGHT=${TIMEOUT_PREFLIGHT:-300}
TIMEOUT_GATE=${TIMEOUT_GATE:-2400}
TIMEOUT_SOAK=${TIMEOUT_SOAK:-$((SOAK_MINUTES * 60 + 900))}   # the soak, plus 15 min for setup and teardown
TIMEOUT_BENCHMARK=${TIMEOUT_BENCHMARK:-3600}
TIMEOUT_MUTATION=${TIMEOUT_MUTATION:-5400}
TIMEOUT_DEPLOYMENT=${TIMEOUT_DEPLOYMENT:-900}
TIMEOUT_RUNTIME=${TIMEOUT_RUNTIME:-1800}
TIMEOUT_DASHBOARD=${TIMEOUT_DASHBOARD:-900}
TIMEOUT_BOUNDARY=${TIMEOUT_BOUNDARY:-600}
TIMEOUT_INTEGRATION=${TIMEOUT_INTEGRATION:-1800}
TIMEOUT_REPORT=${TIMEOUT_REPORT:-300}
# ⚠️ Tracking's identity scenarios play real video through RTSP in real time — the clock is the
# fixture's, not ours, so this budget is dominated by playback and cannot be shortened by a faster host.
TIMEOUT_TRACKING=${TIMEOUT_TRACKING:-1800}
TIMEOUT_TRACKING_BROWSER=${TIMEOUT_TRACKING_BROWSER:-900}
TIMEOUT_TRACKING_BENCHMARK=${TIMEOUT_TRACKING_BENCHMARK:-3600}
TIMEOUT_TRACKING_MUTATION=${TIMEOUT_TRACKING_MUTATION:-3600}
# ⚠️ The bridge stages also run real video in real time, and three of them wait out deliberate
# outages — twenty seconds of broker-down, twenty of camera-off, twenty of camera-back. Those waits
# are the measurement, so a faster host does not shorten them either.
TIMEOUT_EVENT_BRIDGE=${TIMEOUT_EVENT_BRIDGE:-1200}
TIMEOUT_EVENT_REPLAY=${TIMEOUT_EVENT_REPLAY:-900}
TIMEOUT_EVENT_BROWSER=${TIMEOUT_EVENT_BROWSER:-900}
# ⚠️ Assignment stages wait on a 5 s poll cycle converging twice per change, so they are slower than
# their work suggests. The mutation budget is the largest in the file: eight breaks, six of which
# rebuild an image.
TIMEOUT_ASSIGNMENT=${TIMEOUT_ASSIGNMENT:-1500}
TIMEOUT_ASSIGNMENT_RUNTIME=${TIMEOUT_ASSIGNMENT_RUNTIME:-1500}
TIMEOUT_ASSIGNMENT_BROWSER=${TIMEOUT_ASSIGNMENT_BROWSER:-900}
TIMEOUT_ASSIGNMENT_BENCHMARK=${TIMEOUT_ASSIGNMENT_BENCHMARK:-2400}
TIMEOUT_ASSIGNMENT_MUTATION=${TIMEOUT_ASSIGNMENT_MUTATION:-7200}
# ⚠️ P-8 Phase 7. Loitering needs a window several observation intervals long — the platform samples
# a continuously present subject about once per event-dedup bucket (~10 s), so a run shorter than a
# minute cannot accumulate a threshold and would time out on the product working correctly.
TIMEOUT_LOITERING=${TIMEOUT_LOITERING:-1500}
TIMEOUT_LOITERING_ZONES=${TIMEOUT_LOITERING_ZONES:-300}
TIMEOUT_RULE_BROWSER=${TIMEOUT_RULE_BROWSER:-900}
TIMEOUT_RULE_BENCHMARK=${TIMEOUT_RULE_BENCHMARK:-3600}
TIMEOUT_RULE_MUTATION=${TIMEOUT_RULE_MUTATION:-9000}
TIMEOUT_PUBLISHER_BENCHMARK=${TIMEOUT_PUBLISHER_BENCHMARK:-3600}
# Six mutations, three of which rebuild the media image twice each (apply, then restore).
TIMEOUT_BRIDGE_MUTATION=${TIMEOUT_BRIDGE_MUTATION:-5400}
TIMEOUT_BROKER_RESILIENCE=${TIMEOUT_BROKER_RESILIENCE:-1800}

# ── pre-flight thresholds ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
MIN_FREE_GB=${MIN_FREE_GB:-25}   # image rebuilds in the mutation stage need real room
REQUIRE_CLEAN_TREE=${REQUIRE_CLEAN_TREE:-true}   # ⚠️ see the mutation warning — do not set false for an unattended run
REQUIRED_CONTAINERS=${REQUIRED_CONTAINERS:-"vip-prod-gateway-1 vip-prod-media-1 vip-prod-inference-1 vip-prod-console-1"}

# ── regression thresholds, used by the report ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A trend is only a regression if it crosses one of these against the previous successful run.
REGRESS_P95_PERCENT=${REGRESS_P95_PERCENT:-20}
REGRESS_MEMORY_PERCENT=${REGRESS_MEMORY_PERCENT:-15}
REGRESS_DROP_POINTS=${REGRESS_DROP_POINTS:-3}   # percentage points of frame loss at the sustainable rung

# ── where output goes ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Kept under scripts/ so everything about unattended verification lives in one place: the commands,
# the engine, and the evidence they produce.
#
# ⚠️ Both are DEDICATED subdirectories, and that matters — `cleanup.sh --prune` deletes whole run
# directories, so it must never be pointed at a folder that also holds source. Keep these paths
# exclusive to generated output.
#
# For "just show me last night": scripts/reports/nightly/latest/summary.md always points at the
# newest run, and scripts/reports/nightly/INDEX.md is the one-table history of every run.
REPORT_ROOT=${REPORT_ROOT:-scripts/reports/nightly}
LOG_ROOT=${LOG_ROOT:-scripts/logs/nightly}

# ── behaviour ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
KEEP_AWAKE=${KEEP_AWAKE:-true}   # wrap the run in `caffeinate` — without it a sleeping Mac ends the night early
KEEP_RUNS=${KEEP_RUNS:-30}   # `scripts/cleanup.sh` prunes report directories older than this many days

# ── which mutations to run (empty = all seven) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⚠️ The mutation suite EDITS REAL SOURCE FILES and REBUILDS REAL IMAGES. It restores from a byte
# snapshot in a `finally` and the engine restores again if the run is killed — but it is the only
# thing here that writes to your working tree. Never run it with uncommitted work in flight; that is
# what REQUIRE_CLEAN_TREE enforces.
MUTATIONS=${MUTATIONS:-""}
